    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d34">

                <pb facs="0109" n="62" xml:id="pg62"/>

                <head>34. Memorandum From Dennis C. Blair of the National Security Council Staff to
                    the President’s Assistant for National Security Affairs (Clark)<anchor n="1"
                        xml:id="d34-f1"/>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>Washington</placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1982-04-01">April 1, 1982</date>
                    </dateline>
                </opener>
                <list subtype="subject" type="description">
                    <label>SUBJECT</label>
                    <item>U.K.-Argentine Dispute in Falkland Islands</item>
                </list>
                <p>In briefing the President this morning,<anchor n="2" xml:id="d34-f2"/> the
                    following points are most important:</p>
                <p>—Last night Mrs. Thatcher asked you to call General Galtieri to ask him for an
                    assurance that he will not authorize an invasion of the Falkland Islands;<anchor
                        n="3" xml:id="d34-f3"/>
                </p>
                <p>—An amphibious force, probably carrying 500 troops, is on the way to the Falkland
                    Islands, and is estimated to arrive at 6:00 a.m. on Friday;<anchor n="4"
                        xml:id="d34-f4"/>
                </p>
                <p>—Secretary Haig has instructed our ambassador to tell General Galtieri that any
                    military action would wreck the promising U.S.-Argentina relationship;<anchor
                        n="5" xml:id="d34-f5"/>
                </p>
                <p>The question to decide this morning is whether the President should personally
                    call Galtieri to urge restraint.</p>
                <p>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">Pros</hi>
                </p>
                <p>—Mrs. Thatcher has asked the President to do so;</p>
                <p>—The call might cause Galtieri to think twice about the invasion and call it off
                    or delay it;</p>
                <p>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">Cons</hi>
                </p>
                <p>—The President runs the risk of becoming a mediator in an intractable dispute
                    which has gone on for years;</p>
                <p>—We do not yet have the results of the ambassador’s call on Galtieri,<anchor
                        n="6" xml:id="d34-f6"/> so it may not be necessary for the President to
                    intervene personally</p>
                <pb facs="0110" n="63" xml:id="pg63"/>
                <p>The question for the longer term is the attitude the United States should take if
                    the Argentinians invade tomorrow, and war breaks out between the two
                    countries.</p>
                <p>—We clearly should call for an end to hostilities, and support peace conferences,
                    cease-fires, etc.</p>
                <p>—The United Kingdom is both in the right, and a more important and closer ally.
                    In the final analysis, we must support the U.K.</p>
                <p>—We should consider initiatives like a personal envoy from the President (a la
                        Habib)<anchor n="7" xml:id="d34-f7"/>
                </p>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d34-f1" n="1" type="source" xml:id="d34-f1-content">
                <p>Source: Reagan Library, Executive Secretariat, NSC Country File, Latin
                    America/General, Argentina (01/01/1982–04/02/1982). Top Secret. The date is
                    handwritten. Poindexter initialed the top right-hand corner of the
                    memorandum.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d34-f2" n="2" xml:id="d34-f2-content">
                <p>Most likely a reference to the President’s daily national security briefing. On
                    April 1, Reagan met with Bush, Clark, Gregg, and Meese for the briefing in the
                    Oval Office from 9:30 to 10:10 a.m. (Reagan Library, President’s Daily Diary) No
                    other record of the briefing has been found.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d34-f3" n="3" xml:id="d34-f3-content">
                <p>See <ref target="#d28">Document 28</ref>.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d34-f4" n="4" xml:id="d34-f4-content">
                <p>April 2.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d34-f5" n="5" xml:id="d34-f5-content">
                <p>See <ref target="#d31">Document 31</ref>.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d34-f6" n="6" xml:id="d34-f6-content">
                <p>See <ref target="#d37">Document 37</ref>.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d34-f7" n="7" xml:id="d34-f7-content">
                <p>Reference is to Ambassador Philip C. Habib. Following his retirement from the
                    Foreign Service, Habib served as a special envoy on behalf of the Carter and
                    Reagan administrations, most notably in Lebanon.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
